  "code": "2845",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.9811%; height: 133.889px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 50.001%;\"><col style=\"width: 50.001%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>Result In U/mL&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Remarks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>&lt; 12&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>12 &ndash; 18&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Equivocal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.4722px;\">\n<td>&gt; 18&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Comments </strong>:<br>Antibodies to Soluble liver antigen (SLA) are specific for Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH) and present in 20% of these patients,<br>many of whom are negative for other autoantibodies. AIH is a chronic progressive liver disease of unknown origin that<br>responds well to immunosuppressive therapy and has a poor prognosis if untreated . Early and accurate diagnosis is hence<br>of great importance. 70% of these patients also show presence of Anti nuclear antibodies, Anti smooth muscle antibodies<br>and Liver Kidney microsomal antibodies which are of diagnostic value for AIH but not specific for the disease since<br>they also occur in 10-15% patients with Viral hepatitis and other immune mediated diseases</p>",
